## Summary
Multi-line blockquotes lose their quote after the first line when OpenClaw delivers to Slack. Markdown such as `> line one\n> line two\n> line three` reaches `chat.postMessage` as `> line one\nline two\nline three`. Slack mrkdwn applies `>` to a single line only, so in the Slack client just the first line is quoted and every following line renders as ordinary text. The same loss hits a quote that is carried into a later message chunk: the continuation chunk starts with no `>` at all. Agents quote multi-line content (error output, user messages, excerpts) often, so the quote boundary is routinely wrong in Slack replies, `message send`, block section text, and draft previews.

## Root cause
`extensions/slack/src/format.ts` parses Markdown with `blockquotePrefix: "> "` and renders the IR directly. The shared Markdown IR emits that prefix once, at `blockquote_open`, so only the first line of the quote carries it:

```ts
// before
function prepareSlackMarkdownIR(markdown: string, options: SlackMarkdownOptions): MarkdownIR {
  return makeSlackEmphasisStylesSafe(
    markdownToIR(markdown ?? "", {
      ...
      blockquotePrefix: "> ",
      ...
    }),
  );
}
...
renderMarkdownWithMarkers(ir, buildSlackRenderOptions(options), SLACK_FORMAT_PROFILE)
...
renderChunk: (chunk) => {
  const rendered = renderMarkdownWithMarkers(chunk, renderOptions, SLACK_FORMAT_PROFILE);
```

Slack's documented block quote syntax is line-scoped ("add the > character at the beginning of one or more lines"), so the IR's single leading prefix is not enough for this target.

## Fix
A Slack-owned `quoteSlackBlockquoteLines` step runs on the IR right before both render sites (`normalizeSlackOutboundText` and each `markdownToSlackMrkdwnChunks` chunk). It inserts `> ` at the start of every non-empty line inside a `blockquote` span, and at offset 0 when a chunk begins inside a quote, using the existing `applyMarkdownTextEdits` helper. Style, link, annotation, and list marker offsets are remapped so emphasis, links, and task-list fallbacks stay attached to the same text. Newlines inside `code_block` spans are left alone, so fenced code keeps its exact content.

```ts
// after
renderMarkdownWithMarkers(
  quoteSlackBlockquoteLines(ir),
  buildSlackRenderOptions(options),
  SLACK_FORMAT_PROFILE,
)
...
renderChunk: (chunk) => {
  const rendered = renderMarkdownWithMarkers(
    quoteSlackBlockquoteLines(chunk),
    renderOptions,
    SLACK_FORMAT_PROFILE,
  );
```

## Why this is the right boundary
The line-scoped quote rule is a property of Slack mrkdwn, and `format.ts` is the single Slack renderer every outbound text path goes through (`send.ts`, monitor replies, reply blocks, draft stream, channel actions, progress cards). Running the step after IR chunking means the chunker still measures the rendered length (the added `> ` counts against the limit) and each chunk is quoted on its own. The shared Markdown IR is untouched, so other channels that use `blockquotePrefix` keep their current output; WhatsApp mrkdwn has the same line-scoped quote syntax and can adopt the same treatment separately. The existing escaping in `escapeSlackMrkdwnText` already preserves a leading `> ` on each line, so mentions, entities, and link tokens on quoted lines are escaped exactly as before. Quotes containing fenced code keep their previous output.

## Verification
- `extensions/slack/src/format.test.ts` adds two cases: a multi-line quote with bold text, a blank quoted line and a list renders `> ` on every quoted line while text after the quote stays unquoted, a quote around fenced code keeps the code untouched and quotes the following line; and `markdownToSlackMrkdwnChunks` keeps `> ` on a quote line carried into the second chunk. Both fail on pristine main and pass with this patch (`node scripts/run-vitest.mjs extensions/slack/src/format.test.ts`, 47 passed).
- `oxfmt --check` clean on both changed files.
- Local tsgo and oxlint were not run per the contributor workflow for this batch; GitHub Actions covers them.

## Real behavior proof
Behavior addressed: A multi-line Markdown blockquote sent to Slack arrives at `chat.postMessage` with `>` only on its first line, so Slack shows every following line unquoted.
Real environment tested: The Slack plugin's production outbound adapter (`slackOutbound.sendText` from `extensions/slack/src/outbound-adapter.ts`) driven under `node --import tsx` with a real config (`channels.slack.botToken`), real account resolution, real `sendMessageSlack`, real `markdownToSlackMrkdwnChunks` rendering and the real `@slack/web-api` WebClient. Only the Slack Web API host was replaced, via the plugin's supported `SLACK_API_URL` override, with a loopback HTTP server on 127.0.0.1 that logs each `chat.postMessage` body and returns `ok: true`. The CLI `openclaw message send` path could not load the Slack plugin from source on this machine (`ERR_PACKAGE_PATH_NOT_EXPORTED` for `unicorn-magic` via `npm-run-path`, an environment issue unrelated to this change), so the adapter entry it calls was driven directly. Pristine main 52e113b095 first, then this patch, identical input.
Exact steps or command run after this patch: `SLACK_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:27431/api/ OPENCLAW_STATE_DIR=<tmp> node --import tsx drive.mts`, where `drive.mts` calls `slackOutbound.sendText({ cfg, to: "channel:C0123456789", accountId: "default", text })` with `text` = `Quoting the incident report:\n\n> The deploy failed at 14:02.\n> Rollback finished at 14:09.\n> No customer data was affected.\n\nNext steps below.`
Evidence after fix:
```
# BEFORE (pristine main 52e113b095)
"messageId":"1700000000.000100"
[slack-api] chat.postMessage {"channel":"C0123456789","text":"Quoting the incident report:\n\n> The deploy failed at 14:02.\nRollback finished at 14:09.\nNo customer data was affected.\n\nNext steps below."}
# AFTER (this patch, identical inputs)
"messageId":"1700000000.000100"
[slack-api] chat.postMessage {"channel":"C0123456789","text":"Quoting the incident report:\n\n> The deploy failed at 14:02.\n> Rollback finished at 14:09.\n> No customer data was affected.\n\nNext steps below."}
```
Observed result after fix: On main the posted text drops the quote marker from the second and third quoted lines, so Slack renders them as plain text; with the patch all three quoted lines carry `> ` while the intro and trailing lines stay unquoted.
What was not tested: No delivery to a real Slack workspace (the Web API was a loopback stand-in), so rendering in the Slack client was not observed directly and relies on Slack's documented per-line quote syntax; the CLI entry point was not exercised because of the local plugin-load environment issue noted above; full build not run.

## What Problem This Solves

Automatic recall can continue treating a memory file as trusted after a tool replaces it with identical text but records its origin as untrusted.

## User Impact

Project-memory context uses the updated classification. Explicit search remains available.

## Why This Change Was Made

Refresh provenance even when the text hash and cached index are unchanged. Read the current classification after waiting for the database writer, so a queued update cannot restore an older classification. Reuse indexed content and vectors without new document embedding requests.

## Evidence

CLI agent runs capture trusted memory in the final provider request, then exclude it after `web_fetch` and an identical-text `apply_patch` replacement. Explicit `memory_search` still returns the note. Model replies came from a local scripted provider.

Regressions cover ordinary and batch indexing, queued downgrades, SQLite retries, and valid promotions. The recorded release-state run keeps v2026.10.1 memory searchable after the existing migrations, preserving content, embedding values, settings, and provenance.

After merging main, the provenance and publication tests and the real SQLite retry checks pass. Type, lint, and memory-core package checks also pass. Standalone `config validate` remains unverified after an earlier `Shared-state read and cleanup failed`; the recorded memory CLI checks pass.

