## Summary
A Telegram sticker sent through the shared `message` tool with `silent: true` still notifies every recipient. `silent` is a shared message-tool parameter and the Telegram `send`, `poll`, and location paths all map it to `disable_notification`, but the `sticker` action drops it, so the Bot API `sendSticker` request goes out without `disable_notification`.

## Root cause
`extensions/telegram/src/action-runtime.ts` reads `replyTo` and `threadId` for the `sendSticker` action but never `silent`, and `sendStickerTelegram` in `extensions/telegram/src/send-special.ts` only accepts `TelegramThreadedSendOpts`, which has no `silent` field, so the request params carry only the thread params:

```ts
// before
const replyToMessageId = readTelegramReplyToMessageId(params);
const messageThreadId = readTelegramThreadId(params);
const token = requireToken();
const result = await sendStickerTelegram(to, fileId, {
  ...apiOptions,
  token,
  replyToMessageId,
  messageThreadId,
});

// send-special.ts
const stickerParams =
  Object.keys(prepared.threadParams).length > 0 ? prepared.threadParams : undefined;
```

## Fix
The action reads `silent` once, next to `accountId`, for every send-like action (it was already read separately by `sendMessage` and `poll`), and passes it to `sendStickerTelegram`. `sendStickerTelegram` accepts `silent` and adds `disable_notification` the same way `sendPollTelegram` does:

```ts
// after
const silent = readBooleanParam(params, "silent");
...
const result = await sendStickerTelegram(to, fileId, {
  ...apiOptions,
  token,
  replyToMessageId,
  messageThreadId,
  silent,
});

// send-special.ts
const stickerParams = {
  ...prepared.threadParams,
  ...(opts.silent === true ? { disable_notification: true } : {}),
};
```

## Why this is the right boundary
`sendStickerTelegram` owns the Bot API `sendSticker` request, so it is where `disable_notification` belongs, matching `sendPollTelegram` and the text/location senders. The action runtime is the only caller of `sendStickerTelegram` and already owns reading the shared tool params. Reading `silent` once at the top removes the two existing per-branch copies instead of adding a third; `readBooleanParam` never throws, so `sendMessage` and `poll` behave exactly as before. Without `silent`, the sticker request is unchanged (`chat_id`, `sticker`, and the thread params only). Discord fixed the same gap for its sticker sends in #129479.

## Verification
- `extensions/telegram/src/action-runtime.test.ts`: new case "sends silent stickers without a notification" drives the registered `telegramPlugin.actions.handleAction` `sticker` action against the existing loopback Bot API fixture and asserts the single `sendSticker` request is `{ chat_id: "-1001", sticker: "sticker-file", disable_notification: true }`. It fails on pristine main (missing `disable_notification`) and passes with this patch; the whole file passes (37 tests) with the patch.
- `oxfmt --check` is clean on the three changed files.
- Local tsgo and oxlint were not run per the contributor workflow for this batch; GitHub Actions covers them.

## Real behavior proof
Behavior addressed: the Telegram `sticker` message action ignored `silent: true`, so silent stickers still sent a notification, while `poll` with the same flag was silent.
Real environment tested: The production Telegram message-action adapter (`telegramMessageActions.handleAction` from `extensions/telegram/src/channel-actions.ts`, the same object the channel plugin registers) run from source with `node --import tsx`, real config (`botToken`, `actions.sticker: true`, `apiRoot` pointed at a local HTTP server), real grammY client and real HTTP requests. The only stand-in is the Telegram Bot API itself: a loopback HTTP server on 127.0.0.1 that records each request body and answers like Telegram. Run on pristine main 6794f68478b and on this patch with identical inputs.
Exact steps or command run after this patch: `OPENCLAW_STATE_DIR=<tmp> node --import tsx drive-sticker.mts log.jsonl`, which calls `handleAction` for `{ action: "poll", to: "-1001234567890", pollQuestion: "Lunch?", pollOption: ["Yes","No"], silent: true }` and then `{ action: "sticker", to: "-1001234567890", stickerId: ["CAACAgIAAxkBAAEquiet"], silent: true }`, then prints `disable_notification` from each request the Bot API server received.
