## What Problem This Solves

Large SQLite conversations can have a short active suffix after reset or compaction but still pay history-sized cold model-context navigation costs. This bounds eligible acquisition **in the existing history reader Worker**, preserving exact selected transcript bytes.

## User Impact And Scope

Eligible Worker reads certify the semantic suffix from the current projection and seed the existing page owner. Native byte/event limits, tool pairing, admission fences and returned context bytes remain authoritative. Unknown session keys, ambiguous ancestry, historical/retained anchors, opaque prefixes, excessive metadata searches and PostgreSQL retain canonical behavior. This is not a universal history-read bound or an every-turn/full-Gateway latency claim.

The projected helper returns `undefined` on the main thread before reading projection facts or executing SQL. Deprecated synchronous `SessionManager.openModelContext` keeps its canonical synchronous host fallback, values and completion contract; it does **not** receive the Worker optimization. No new cache, configuration, schema, durability or retention policy is introduced. The unrelated inherited warm artifact-provider policy is unchanged.

Related but distinct: #184388 changes warm multi-session retention; this PR reduces eligible cold acquisition. #169723 concerns bounded-open correctness rather than this acquisition work.

## Review Repair

This updates existing #184750, not a replacement PR. The seven-file repair closes the extracted wrapper dependency, accounts for two existing receiver-forwarding benchmark suppressions, makes the benchmark run in an actual Worker, protects the host fallback in the existing parity fixture, and regenerates the canonical SQL-access inventory. Classification is scoped to the guarded operation, not the whole mixed module. There is no SQL baseline increase or ratchet exemption: T1 remains **168 → 168**, deprecated compatibility **15 → 15**.

Main was integrated only to resolve the generated SQL-inventory conflict. The canonical generator was rerun for this integrated source snapshot. All three determining production owners are byte-identical to the prior signed repair; shared main dependencies changed, so the focused gates and final-head runtime were freshly executed rather than relabeled.

The smallest production repair is the import plus early main-thread return. Existing page/accessor owners remain unchanged from the prior candidate; no second owner or warm-cache policy repair is added.

