issues.body:
As far as I can tell, when a headless SOP step calls `send_message_to_peer`, the recipient agent's turn runs without the step's tool scope: the scope is carried in a task-local, and the tool starts the recipient's turn in a detached task without capturing it. The sibling tools `delegate` and `spawn_subagent` do carry the scope into the turns they start.

### Affected component

tools

### Severity

S2 - degraded behavior

### Current behavior

A headless SOP step runs through `agent::run` with the scope in `AgentRunOverrides` and also publishes the same scope as a task-local: `crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/sop/executor.rs:1063` (anchor `sop_step_scope: Some(scope),`) and `:1083` (anchor `with_active_headless_step_scope(`). The turn loop re-reads the task-local on every iteration and merges the step's exclusions into the turn's excluded tools (`crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/agent/turn/mod.rs:1916`, anchor `active_headless_step_scope().map(|scope| {`).

In `send_message_to_peer`, the in-process branch starts the recipient's turn in a detached task:

- `crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/tools/send_message_to_peer.rs:333`, anchor `zeroclaw_spawn::spawn!(async move {`
- `:336`, anchor `crate::agent::loop_::process_message_shared_with_live_config_and_admission_and_principal(`, with `TurnOrigin::AgentDirect` (`:342`) and `InternalPrincipal::PeerAgent` (`:344`).

A task-local does not cross a spawn; `crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/sop/active_scope.rs:82-86` says so (anchor `A task-local does not cross \`spawn\``). The same block already works around this for the cost context (`send_message_to_peer.rs:321-326`, anchor `a detached \`zeroclaw_spawn::spawn!\` task does not inherit the caller's`), but nothing captures the step scope. Searching `send_message_to_peer.rs` for `headless_step_scope|sop_step_scope|active_scope` gives 0 matches, while the same file does match `spawn!` at `:333`, so the search sees the file.

The entry point the tool calls has no parameter for a scope (`process_message_inner`, `crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/agent/loop_.rs:3613-3622`), and it builds the recipient's registry itself with `caller_allowed: None` (`loop_.rs:3788`). Inside the recipient's turn, `active_headless_step_scope()` returns `None` (`active_scope.rs:105-107`). So the recipient is offered its own tool surface (45 tools in the run below), not the step's.

The sibling tools do carry it:
- `delegate`: captures before the spawn and restores inside it (`delegate.rs:3377` / `:3416` for background delegation, `:3717` / `:3837` for parallel fan-out), and filters the target's registry (`delegate.rs:5059-5073`, anchor `Handing work to another agent is not a way to`, a comment that continues on the next line).
- `spawn_subagent`: `crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/tools/spawn_subagent.rs:98`, anchor `sop_step_scope: crate::sop::active_scope::active_headless_step_scope(),`.

When the SOP runtime is enabled, the recipient's turn also builds its own SOP engine (`loop_.rs:3737`, anchor `if config.sop.runtime_enabled() {`) and `all_tools_with_runtime...` registers `sop_execute`, `sop_advance` and `sop_approve` when an engine is present (`crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/tools/mod.rs:2717-2756`). The step turn always drops those three (`active_scope.rs:11`, `SOP_CONTROL_TOOLS`). I did not run that part: my reproduction below does not enable the SOP runtime, so the control tools do not appear in it.

### Expected behavior

The code says a run started from inside a headless step keeps the step's boundary:

- `crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/sop/active_scope.rs:97-103`: "A tool that starts a child agent run passes this into the child's `AgentRunOverrides`: spawning a child does not widen the step's capability boundary".
- `crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/agent/loop_.rs:1430-1437` (doc of `sop_step_scope`): "Also `Some` on a child run started from inside such a step, which inherits the step's boundary rather than rebuilding the agent's full surface."
- `crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/agent/turn/mod.rs:1909-1911`: reading the scope from the task "also covers the loops this one drives on behalf of another agent: a delegated target assembles its own registry, and inherits the boundary all the same."

The user docs make the same promise for child agents: `docs/book/src/sop/fan-in/cron.md:18` ("A child agent the step spawns inherits the same exclusions, so spawning one is not a way around the step's boundary") and `docs/book/src/sop/fan-in/overview.md:13`. They do not mention peers, so whether a peer turn counts as a "child agent" is a question for maintainers. `docs/book/src/developing/tool-inventory.md:68` groups `send_message_to_peer` with `spawn_subagent` and `delegate` as delegation that "must share risk profiles, tools, memory, and parent/child constraints". One line points the other way: `tool-inventory.md:209` lists what each of the three carries into the turn it starts, naming "its security policy and SOP step scope for `spawn_subagent`", delegation policy and ceilings for `delegate`, and "its identity as the peer turn's principal for `send_message_to_peer`". It does not name the step scope for `delegate` either, although `delegate` does carry it (`delegate.rs:3377`, `:3416`), so I read that line as not exhaustive. If carrying the step scope into peer turns is intentionally not part of the design, that is what I would like to know.

A minimal fix would be to capture `active_headless_step_scope()` before the `spawn!` in `send_message_to_peer.rs` and wrap the recipient's turn future with `with_inherited_headless_step_scope(...)`, as `delegate` does. I applied exactly that locally to check the test below: it then passes, and the recipient is offered only `send_message_to_peer`. I have not run any other test with it. Since open draft PRs (#11230, #11410, #11526) also edit this file, I would rather wait for maintainer guidance on where this should land before opening a PR.

### Steps to reproduce

```bash
# Tool-level reproduction on master @ 1c1cd118ac536a1dd1bc79b9cce69cbdc04e4f29.
# 1. Append the test below to `mod tests` in
#    crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/tools/send_message_to_peer.rs
# 2. Run:
CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0 cargo test -p zeroclaw-runtime --lib --locked -j 1 \
  zz_peer_turn_in_headless_sop_step_scope_probe -- --test-threads=1 --nocapture
# Expected (contract): the recipient's request offers only `send_message_to_peer`.
# Observed: it offers 45 tools; 44 are outside the step's scope.
```

Setup the test uses: sender agent `sender` and recipient agent `recipient`, both in one peer group on `telegram.prod`; the recipient's provider is a local OpenAI-compatible mock that records the request bodies. The test installs a step scope (`allow = ["send_message_to_peer"]`, `step_scope_enforce = true`) as the task-local, the way the headless driver does (`executor.rs:1083`), calls the tool's `execute()` inside it, and then inspects the first request the recipient's turn sends to the mock. A precondition asserts that the request carried native tool specs at all.

```rust
    #[tokio::test]
    async fn zz_peer_turn_in_headless_sop_step_scope_probe() {
        use crate::control_plane::ControlPlaneHandle;
        use axum::{Json, Router, extract::State, routing::post};
        use std::collections::HashMap;
        use zeroclaw_config::schema::{
            ModelProviderConfig, OllamaModelProviderConfig, RiskProfileConfig, SopConfig,
        };

        type Bodies = Arc<Mutex<Vec<serde_json::Value>>>;
        async fn capture(
            State(bodies): State<Bodies>,
            Json(body): Json<serde_json::Value>,
        ) -> Json<serde_json::Value> {
            bodies.lock().push(body);
            Json(json!({
                "choices": [{"message": {"content": "ok"}}],
                "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 1, "completion_tokens": 1}
            }))
        }

        let bodies: Bodies = Arc::new(Mutex::new(Vec::new()));
        let listener = tokio::net::TcpListener::bind("127.0.0.1:0")
            .await
            .expect("bind");
        let addr = listener.local_addr().expect("addr");
        let app = Router::new()
            .route("/v1/chat/completions", post(capture))
            .with_state(bodies.clone());
        let server = zeroclaw_spawn::spawn!(async move {
            axum::serve(listener, app).await.expect("serve");
        });

        let workspace = tempfile::TempDir::new().expect("temp data dir");
        let mut config = Config {
            data_dir: workspace.path().to_path_buf(),
            config_path: workspace.path().join("config.toml"),
            ..Config::default()
        };
        config.providers.models.ollama.insert(
            "default".to_string(),
            OllamaModelProviderConfig {
                base: ModelProviderConfig {
                    model: Some("mock-model".to_string()),
                    uri: Some(format!("http://{addr}")),
                    timeout_secs: Some(5),
                    pricing: HashMap::new(),
                    ..ModelProviderConfig::default()
                },
                ..OllamaModelProviderConfig::default()
            },
        );
        config.agents.insert(
            "sender".to_string(),
            AliasedAgentConfig {
                channels: vec!["telegram.prod".into()],
                ..AliasedAgentConfig::default()
            },
        );
        config.agents.insert(
            "recipient".to_string(),
            AliasedAgentConfig {
                channels: vec!["telegram.prod".into()],
                model_provider: "ollama.default".into(),
                risk_profile: "default".into(),
                ..AliasedAgentConfig::default()
            },
        );
        config.peer_groups.insert(
            "ops".to_string(),
            PeerGroupConfig {
                channel: "telegram".into(),
                agents: vec![AgentAlias::new("sender"), AgentAlias::new("recipient")],
                ..PeerGroupConfig::default()
            },
        );
        config
            .risk_profiles
            .insert("default".to_string(), RiskProfileConfig::default());

        // The step may only message a peer; everything else is scoped out.
        let scope = crate::sop::active_scope::HeadlessStepScope {
            run_id: "run-1".into(),
            step: crate::sop::SopStep {
                number: 1,
                scope: Some(crate::sop::StepToolScope {
                    allow: Some(vec!["send_message_to_peer".into()]),
                    deny: Vec::new(),
                }),
                ..crate::sop::SopStep::default()
            },
            config: SopConfig {
                step_scope_enforce: true,
                ..SopConfig::default()
            },
        };

        let control_plane = ControlPlaneHandle::open(workspace.path())
            .expect("isolated control plane should initialize");
        let authority = crate::LiveConfigAuthority::new(config.clone());
        let tool = SendMessageToPeerTool::new_with_capability(
            Arc::new(config.clone()),
            "sender",
            Some(authority.execution_capability()),
        )
        .with_control_plane(control_plane);

        let result = crate::sop::active_scope::with_active_headless_step_scope(
            scope,
            tool.execute(json!({
                "channel": "telegram.prod",
                "target": "recipient",
                "message": "status?"
            })),
        )
        .await
        .expect("execute should accept the peer send");
        assert!(result.success, "{result:?}");

        let mut first = None;
        for _ in 0..200_000 {
            if let Some(body) = bodies.lock().first().cloned() {
                first = Some(body);
                break;
            }
            tokio::task::yield_now().await;
        }
        let body = first.expect("the detached recipient turn must reach the provider");

        let offered: Vec<String> = body["tools"]
            .as_array()
            .map(|tools| {
                tools
                    .iter()
                    .filter_map(|t| t["function"]["name"].as_str().map(str::to_string))
                    .collect()
            })
            .unwrap_or_default();
        eprintln!("PROBE offered tools ({}): {:?}", offered.len(), offered);
        eprintln!(
            "PROBE body keys: {:?}",
            body.as_object().map(|o| o.keys().cloned().collect::<Vec<_>>())
        );
        assert!(
            !offered.is_empty(),
            "precondition: the recipient request carried no native tool specs; body = {body}"
        );
        let outside: Vec<&String> = offered
            .iter()
            .filter(|name| name.as_str() != "send_message_to_peer")
            .collect();
        assert!(
            outside.is_empty(),
            "a peer turn started from inside a scoped headless SOP step was offered tools \
             outside the step scope: {outside:?}"
        );

        server.abort();
    }
```

What this does not show: the test installs the task-local by hand and calls `execute()` directly; it does not run the headless driver, a daemon, or a real SOP with a cron trigger. It measures the tools offered to the provider in the recipient's first request, not a call to an excluded tool. The SOP runtime is not enabled in it, so it does not show the recipient receiving `sop_execute`/`sop_advance`/`sop_approve`; that part rests on reading the code. I did not run the case `step_scope_enforce = false`.

### Impact

- The step's declared tool scope (under `sop.step_scope_enforce`) and, with the SOP runtime enabled, the exclusion of the SOP control tools do not reach the turn the step starts through `send_message_to_peer`. The recipient is bounded only by its own configuration. I assume that is its own agent policy and did not re-verify which policy the recipient's turn runs under.
- Preconditions: SOP runtime enabled; a headless SOP step whose scope does not exclude `send_message_to_peer` (or `step_scope_enforce` off); the step's agent shares a peer group with another agent.
- Frequency: when the step calls the tool with an agent recipient, as in the one case I measured (tool level, in-process branch). I did not run the external-peer branch.
- The message text is chosen by the step's model, so what the recipient does with the unscoped surface depends on its model and prompt.

Related, as far as I can tell not a duplicate: #9841 (merged; commit `2f5e85041`) introduced `with_inherited_headless_step_scope` and its use in `delegate` and `spawn_subagent`; #10813 (merged) removed the SOP control tools from headless step turns; #11045 (merged) rewrote this block of `send_message_to_peer.rs` and kept the detached spawn; #11577 (open) makes live nested steps publish their step scope as a task-local too, so that `spawn_subagent` and `delegate` read it there (at this SHA `agent/turn/mod.rs` has no `with_active_headless_step_scope`, so as far as I can tell live steps carry nothing into those tools today, and `send_message_to_peer` would still miss it after that PR). I only looked at headless steps. None of the diffs of #11230, #11410, #11526, #11174 and #11577 that I downloaded on 2026-10-31 adds a reference to the step scope inside `send_message_to_peer.rs`. #12167 reports a different omission in the same tool (the sender's autonomy level and action budget are not checked); the fixes touch different places.

### Logs / stack traces

```text
test tools::send_message_to_peer::tests::zz_peer_turn_in_headless_sop_step_scope_probe ... PROBE offered tools (45): ["TodoWrite", "ask_user", "backup", "calculator", "canvas", "channel_room", "content_search", "cron_add", ..., "send_message_to_peer", "send_via", ..., "shell", "spawn_subagent", "web_fetch"]

thread 'tools::send_message_to_peer::tests::zz_peer_turn_in_headless_sop_step_scope_probe' panicked at crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/tools/send_message_to_peer.rs:1957:9:
a peer turn started from inside a scoped headless SOP step was offered tools outside the step scope: ["TodoWrite", "ask_user", "backup", ... (44 names, every one except send_message_to_peer)]
test result: FAILED. 0 passed; 1 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 5902 filtered out
```

With the minimal fix applied locally, the same test prints `PROBE offered tools (1): ["send_message_to_peer"]` and passes (`1 passed`).

### ZeroClaw version

master @ 1c1cd118ac536a1dd1bc79b9cce69cbdc04e4f29 (`git describe`: v0.8.7-28-g1c1cd118a)

### Rust version

rustc 1.98.1 (48a229cea 2026-09-01)

### Operating system

Ubuntu 24.04.4 LTS on WSL2 (kernel 6.6.87.2-microsoft-standard-WSL2)

### Regression?

Unknown

### Pre-flight checks

- [x] I reproduced this on the latest master branch or latest release. (At tool level, on `1c1cd118a`, `origin/master` when run; the headless driver and a real SOP run were not exercised.)
- [x] I redacted secrets, tokens, and personal data from all submitted content.

As far as I can tell, a `readonly` agent can still start a turn for another agent in its peer group, because `send_message_to_peer` does not consult the sender's autonomy level and does not count against the sender's action budget. The sibling tool `a2a_send` refuses in `readonly` before doing anything, so the two agent-to-agent send paths behave differently.

### Affected component

tools

### Severity

S2 - degraded behavior

### Current behavior

`SendMessageToPeerTool` holds no security policy: its fields are `config`, `live_config`, `sender_alias`, `description`, `task_control_plane` and `execution_capability` (`crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/tools/send_message_to_peer.rs:43-50`, anchor `pub struct SendMessageToPeerTool`). Searching the non-test part of that file (lines 1-781, before `mod tests`) for `SecurityPolicy|security|can_act|enforce_tool_operation|authorize_tool_operation|RateLimited|reserve_action|record_action|AutonomyLevel` gives 0 matches. The same pattern run over `crates/zeroclaw-tools/src/a2a_client.rs` finds `enforce_tool_operation(ToolOperation::Act, "a2a_send")` (line 2019), so the search does see the call when it exists.

The tool is registered with no wrapper: `crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/tools/mod.rs:1776` (anchor `Arc::new(SendMessageToPeerTool::new_with_live_config_and_capability(`), through `register_tool!` and `register_builtin_tool` (`mod.rs:425`), which only push the constructor's result. Note that wrapping in `RateLimitedTool` would not be enough on its own: that wrapper only reserves an action slot (`crates/zeroclaw-tools/src/wrappers.rs:73`, `reserve_action()`) and does not look at `ReadOnly`. In this codebase the read-only block lives inside each tool's `execute`, for example `spawn_subagent.rs:216`, `delegate.rs:2792`, `crates/zeroclaw-tools/src/send_via.rs:301` (`send_via`, the closest sibling that sends to a channel) and `crates/zeroclaw-tools/src/a2a_client.rs:2019`.

In `execute` (`send_message_to_peer.rs:128`) the in-process branch registers a durable `PeerInbox` task (`admit_peer_inbox_task`, line 275; `kind: TaskKind::PeerInbox`, line 457), spawns the recipient's turn (`zeroclaw_spawn::spawn!`, line 333) and returns `success: true` (lines 376-380). The external-peer branch calls `deliver_announcement(...)` (line 383). Neither branch checks the sender's autonomy or reserves an action.

Nothing upstream appears to block it either: `approval_requirement` returns `NotRequired` for `ReadOnly` with the comment `// ReadOnly blocks everything — handled elsewhere; no prompt needed.` (`crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/approval/mod.rs:240`), so the block is expected to come from each tool, and this one does not do it. The `readonly` system prompt also tells the model "Write operations will be rejected by the runtime if attempted" (`crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/agent/prompt.rs:457`). I did not run the full agent loop (see "Steps to reproduce"); that part rests on reading the code.

### Expected behavior

A `readonly` sender should be refused before any effect, with a read-only error, as `a2a_send` is (`crates/zeroclaw-tools/src/a2a_client.rs:3671`, test `a2a_send_denied_in_readonly_autonomy`: it builds `SecurityPolicy { autonomy: AutonomyLevel::ReadOnly, .. }`, expects `!result.success`, an error containing `read-only`, and no network I/O). Sending should also count as an action: `SecurityPolicy::enforce_tool_operation(ToolOperation::Act, ..)` is the existing way to do both (`crates/zeroclaw-config/src/policy.rs:4472`; it calls `authorize_tool_operation`, which rejects when `!can_act()`, then `record_action`).

A minimal fix would be to give the tool a `SecurityPolicy` and call `enforce_tool_operation(ToolOperation::Act, "send_message_to_peer")` at the start of `execute`, before the in-process and external-peer branches, as `a2a_send` does. I have not implemented or tested that. Since open draft PRs (#11230, #11410, #11526) also edit this file, I would rather wait for maintainer guidance on where this should land before opening a PR.

The docs point the same way:
- `docs/book/src/security/autonomy.md:9-11`: `readonly` is "The agent can observe but not change anything. Permitted tools are the ones with no side effects", and the list it gives does not include any messaging tool.
- `docs/book/src/developing/tool-inventory.md:68`: `spawn_subagent`, `delegate` and `send_message_to_peer` "must share risk profiles, tools, memory, and parent/child constraints". `spawn_subagent` and `delegate` call `enforce_tool_operation(ToolOperation::Act, ..)` (`spawn_subagent.rs:216`, `delegate.rs:2792` and `:3120`); `send_message_to_peer` does not.

### Steps to reproduce

```bash
# Tool-level reproduction on master @ 1c1cd118ac536a1dd1bc79b9cce69cbdc04e4f29.
# 1. Append the test below to `mod tests` in
#    crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/tools/send_message_to_peer.rs
# 2. Run:
CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0 cargo test -p zeroclaw-runtime --lib --locked -j 1 \
  zz_readonly_sender_peer_send_probe -- --test-threads=1 --nocapture
# Expected (contract): the first execute() is refused with a "read-only" error.
# Observed: both calls return success=true, and each creates a PeerInbox task row.
```

Setup the test uses: sender agent `sender` on risk profile `ro` (`level = ReadOnly`) and runtime profile `rt` (`max_actions_per_hour = 1`); recipient agent `recipient` on the default profiles; both in one peer group on `telegram.prod`. Before calling the tool, the test asserts that `SecurityPolicy::for_agent(&config, "sender")` reports `can_act() == false` and `max_actions_per_hour == 1`.

```rust
    #[tokio::test]
    async fn zz_readonly_sender_peer_send_probe() {
        use crate::control_plane::ControlPlaneHandle;
        use std::collections::HashMap;
        use zeroclaw_config::autonomy::AutonomyLevel;
        use zeroclaw_config::policy::SecurityPolicy;
        use zeroclaw_config::schema::{
            ModelProviderConfig, OllamaModelProviderConfig, RiskProfileConfig,
        };

        let workspace = tempfile::TempDir::new().expect("temp data dir");
        let mut config = Config {
            data_dir: workspace.path().to_path_buf(),
            config_path: workspace.path().join("config.toml"),
            ..Config::default()
        };
        config.providers.models.ollama.insert(
            "default".to_string(),
            OllamaModelProviderConfig {
                base: ModelProviderConfig {
                    model: Some("mock-model".to_string()),
                    uri: Some("http://127.0.0.1:9".to_string()),
                    timeout_secs: Some(2),
                    pricing: HashMap::new(),
                    ..ModelProviderConfig::default()
                },
                ..OllamaModelProviderConfig::default()
            },
        );
        config.agents.insert(
            "sender".to_string(),
            AliasedAgentConfig {
                channels: vec!["telegram.prod".into()],
                risk_profile: "ro".into(),
                runtime_profile: "rt".into(),
                ..AliasedAgentConfig::default()
            },
        );
        config.agents.insert(
            "recipient".to_string(),
            AliasedAgentConfig {
                channels: vec!["telegram.prod".into()],
                model_provider: "ollama.default".into(),
                risk_profile: "default".into(),
                ..AliasedAgentConfig::default()
            },
        );
        config.peer_groups.insert(
            "ops".to_string(),
            PeerGroupConfig {
                channel: "telegram".into(),
                agents: vec![AgentAlias::new("sender"), AgentAlias::new("recipient")],
                ..PeerGroupConfig::default()
            },
        );
        config
            .risk_profiles
            .insert("default".to_string(), RiskProfileConfig::default());
        config.risk_profiles.insert(
            "ro".to_string(),
            RiskProfileConfig {
                level: AutonomyLevel::ReadOnly,
                ..RiskProfileConfig::default()
            },
        );
        config.runtime_profiles.insert(
            "rt".to_string(),
            zeroclaw_config::schema::RuntimeProfileConfig {
                max_actions_per_hour: 1,
                ..zeroclaw_config::schema::RuntimeProfileConfig::default()
            },
        );

        // Precondition: the sender's own policy is read-only with a budget of 1.
        let policy = SecurityPolicy::for_agent(&config, "sender").expect("sender policy");
        assert!(!policy.can_act(), "precondition: sender policy must be read-only");
        assert_eq!(policy.max_actions_per_hour, 1, "precondition: budget of 1");

        let control_plane = ControlPlaneHandle::open(workspace.path())
            .expect("isolated control plane should initialize");
        let task_store = Arc::clone(&control_plane.store);
        let authority = crate::LiveConfigAuthority::new(config.clone());
        let tool = SendMessageToPeerTool::new_with_capability(
            Arc::new(config.clone()),
            "sender",
            Some(authority.execution_capability()),
        )
        .with_control_plane(control_plane);

        let args = json!({
            "channel": "telegram.prod",
            "target": "recipient",
            "message": "hola"
        });
        let first = tool.execute(args.clone()).await.expect("first execute");
        let second = tool.execute(args).await.expect("second execute");
        eprintln!("PROBE first : success={} output={:?} error={:?}", first.success, first.output, first.error);
        eprintln!("PROBE second: success={} output={:?} error={:?}", second.success, second.output, second.error);

        for (label, res) in [("first", &first), ("second", &second)] {
            let text: String = res.output.to_string();
            let id = text
                .split_once("task_id=")
                .and_then(|(_, v)| v.strip_suffix(')'))
                .map(str::to_string);
            match id {
                Some(id) => {
                    let rec = TaskRegistry::get(task_store.as_ref(), &id).await.expect("store get");
                    eprintln!(
                        "PROBE {label} task row: {:?}",
                        rec.map(|r| (r.kind, r.agent, r.principal_id, r.status))
                    );
                }
                None => eprintln!("PROBE {label}: no task_id in output"),
            }
        }

        // Contract (autonomy.md / a2a_send_denied_in_readonly_autonomy): refuse with a read-only error.
        assert!(!first.success, "read-only sender must be refused; got {first:?}");
        assert!(
            first.error.as_deref().unwrap_or_default().contains("read-only"),
            "error must mention read-only; got {first:?}"
        );
