```

What this does not show: the test calls `execute()` directly and does not run the agent loop, so it does not rule out a gate in the loop that I did not find (I searched the non-test parts of `agent/tool_execution.rs` (lines 1-728), `agent/turn/call_prep.rs` and `agent/turn/approval_gate.rs` for `ReadOnly|can_act(|enforce_tool_operation|authorize_tool_operation` and found no hits; the same pattern does hit in `approval/mod.rs`. That is a pattern search, not a full read of those files, and not a run). The recipient's provider points at a closed port, so the recipient's turn itself fails after acceptance; the test does not wait for it. It also does not read the sender's action counter: the tool holds no policy, so there is no counter it could charge. That the second call is also accepted with a budget of 1 is consistent with that, and the missing field is what the claim rests on.

Control: in the same tree, `cargo test -p zeroclaw-tools --lib --locked -j 1 a2a_send_denied_in_readonly_autonomy -- --test-threads=1` passes (`1 passed`). That test injects the policy directly, so it shows the contract holds for the sibling tool, not that this reproduction's profile-based setup would be refused by it.

### Impact

- An agent whose risk profile is `readonly` can, through this tool, start a full turn for another agent of the same peer group (which may be `supervised` or `full`), or post to a configured external peer, and none of those sends consume its `max_actions_per_hour`.
- Preconditions: the tool is in the sender's registry, and the sender shares a peer group with at least one other agent or has a configured external peer. An operator can hide the tool by name with `excluded_tools` (`SecurityPolicy::is_tool_excluded`, `policy.rs:398`); that is a per-tool opt-out, not a `readonly` check, and as far as I can tell nothing excludes it by default. I did not find a way for the sender to escalate the recipient: the recipient's turn is started with `InternalPrincipal::PeerAgent { sender_alias }` (`send_message_to_peer.rs:344`) and I did not trace which policy it then runs under beyond that.
- Frequency: whenever the preconditions hold, for the in-process peer branch (reproduced at tool level). I did not run the external-peer branch; note that `deliver_announcement` returns `Ok(())` without delivering when no delivery handler is registered (`crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/cron/scheduler.rs:1478-1497`), so whether an external send happens depends on how the binary is wired.
- The messaging text is chosen by the sending model, so the effect is whatever the recipient does with it under its own policy.

Related, with a different axis, so this is not a duplicate as far as I can tell: #12046 (an issue) proposes withholding principal-unaware tools, `send_message_to_peer` among them, from non-admin principals, and the draft PRs #11230 and #11410 add `requires_unrestricted_principal` to this tool; #11526 changes how capabilities reach the recipient's turn; #11307 caps sender-role turns with a per-sender budget and excludes peer sends from budgeted turns; #11667 asks whether the recipient inherits the caller's command policy. None of the diffs of #11230, #11410, #11526, #11307 and #11220 that I downloaded on 2026-10-31 adds a check of `ReadOnly`/`can_act`/`enforce_tool_operation`/`authorize_tool_operation` to `send_message_to_peer`. The issue here is the sender's autonomy level and action budget, which apply even when the caller is an administrator. If maintainers prefer, this can be folded into #12046; I kept it separate because its axis is the principal.

Scope: while checking this I looked at other tools in the registry, and the same question may apply to some of them. I am not listing them here, to keep this report to one reproducible case; I can open a separate design question if that is useful.

### Logs / stack traces

